    bigIdea: 'This is the passage for the man who has found a position he is entitled to take. Asked whether his master pays the tribute, Peter says yes, and Jesus then reasons the liability out with him: kings take custom of strangers rather than of their own children, so "Then are the children free" (Matthew 17:26). He is not liable, and He says so plainly. Then comes the word the whole lesson turns on — "Notwithstanding, lest we should offend them" (Matthew 17:27) — and He pays. Establishing that you do not owe something and deciding whether to insist on it are two separate decisions, and a believer who collapses them into one will win arguments at a cost he never counted.',
