      teen: 'This is a short story about a tax bill, and it turns on one word. Some men who collected the temple tribute came up to Peter and asked whether his teacher paid it. Peter said yes. Then Jesus asks Peter a question. Who do kings collect tax from — their own children, or strangers? Peter says strangers. Obviously. A king does not tax his own family. And Jesus says, "Then are the children free" (Matthew 17:26). Stop there for a second, because He just settled the question. It is His Father temple. He is the Son. The tribute does not apply to Him. He is right and everybody in the story knows it. Now here is the next word, and it is the entire lesson. "Notwithstanding, lest we should offend them, go thou to the sea" (Matthew 17:27). Notwithstanding means even so. Anyway. He was not liable, He knew He was not liable, He said so out loud — and then He paid it. Why? The verse tells you. So as not to put a stumbling block in front of the people collecting. That is such a useful thing to learn early, because most people never separate these two questions. Question one: am I right? Question two: should I push it? Those are different questions with different answers, and almost everybody treats them as one question. You can be completely right and still decide, on purpose, to let it go. Not because you were wrong. Because being right was not the only thing at stake. Think about where this shows up for you. The group project where you genuinely did more than somebody else. The rule you technically did not break. The thing you are owed and could demand. You are allowed to be right and gracious in the same moment. That combination is rare and people notice it. And notice how He paid: He sent Peter fishing, and the money came from a fish. He did not pay out of the group money bag. Nobody else lost anything so He could be gracious. One more piece, because there is a way to get this exactly backwards. Some people read this story and decide the lesson is just give in, avoid conflict, do not argue. That is not it at all, and you can prove it from the verse. He worked out whether He owed it. He reasoned it through with Peter and reached an answer. He did the first job properly. A person who never figures out whether he is right, and just goes along with everything to keep things smooth, has not done what Jesus did here. He has skipped the part Jesus actually did. So do both. Find out whether you are right, honestly. Then decide what to do about it, on purpose. Two decisions. Both of them yours. Neither one skipped.',
      senior: 'Matthew 17:24-27 is a short episode and it carries a distinction that mature stewards need and rarely hear taught. The collectors of the temple tribute approach Peter: "Doth not your master pay tribute?" (Matthew 17:24). Peter answers yes, apparently without consulting Him. Jesus then does something worth naming before the outcome: He reasons the liability out rather than simply deciding it. "Of whom do the kings of the earth take custom or tribute? of their own children, or of strangers?" (Matthew 17:25). Peter gives the obvious answer, and the conclusion follows: "Then are the children free" (Matthew 17:26). Establish that clearly with the class, because the second half means nothing without it. The liability genuinely does not attach. This is His Father house and He is the Son; the analogy is exact and He states the exemption plainly rather than hinting at it. Then the pivot, and the whole session hangs on one word: "Notwithstanding, lest we should offend them" (Matthew 17:27). Notwithstanding — the exemption stands and He sets it aside. And the stated reason is not doubt about the exemption; it is the effect on the people collecting. Now teach the distinction, because this is the transferable part. There are two separate decisions inside every disputed obligation, and most people make only one. The first is a question of FACT and law: do I owe this? The second is a question of JUDGMENT: having established that I do not, do I insist? Collapsing the two is extremely common and it is expensive. A man who finds he is entitled to a position frequently treats the entitlement as the end of the analysis, and then spends credibility, relationships and sometimes a testimony to collect a sum he would not have missed. Make the limits explicit, because this must not become a doctrine of surrender. He did not concede the point — the exemption is on the record in verse 26 and was never withdrawn. He did not pay out of the common purse; the provision came separately, so no one else bore the cost of His graciousness. And the reason was specific and named — avoiding offence to those collecting — not a general reluctance to have a difficult conversation. A steward who yields every position to avoid friction is not imitating this passage; he is avoiding the first decision altogether. Close with the instrument. Each person names one position he is entitled to take and would struggle to explain simply, writes what insisting gains and what it costs in the estimation of a watching neighbour, and then decides deliberately — either way. The deliverable is a decision made on purpose, not a particular outcome.',
