    lesson: 'Israel asked for a king, and Yahweh had the price read to them first. Not a warning in general terms — an itemised schedule, delivered out loud, before consent. "This will be the manner of the king that shall reign over you" (1 Samuel 8:11), and then the list, with one verb repeating until it becomes the theme. "He will take your sons, and appoint them for himself, for his chariots, and to be his horsemen" (1 Samuel 8:11). "And he will take your fields, and your vineyards, and your oliveyards, even the best of them, and give them to his servants" (1 Samuel 8:14). "And he will take the tenth of your seed, and of your vineyards, and give to his officers, and to his servants" (1 Samuel 8:15). "He will take the tenth of your sheep: and ye shall be his servants" (1 Samuel 8:17). Two details in that list are worth stopping on, because a reader moving quickly will take them as rhetoric when they are specifications. The first is the QUALITY clause: not merely the fields, but "even the best of them". A levy assessed against the best of a holding is materially different from one assessed against its average, and it is not an ancient peculiarity — assessments keyed to the highest and best use of a property do exactly this. The second is that the DESTINATION is named. To his servants. To his officers. The take is not abstract revenue disappearing into a treasury; it transfers to identifiable people who now hold what a family held. Then the verse that turns the chapter from a complaint into a teaching. "And ye shall cry out in that day because of your king which ye shall have chosen you; and the LORD will not hear you in that day" (1 Samuel 8:18). Hold the shape of that precisely, because it is uncomfortable and precision is what makes it useful. Disclosure was complete — delivered by a prophet, in detail, in advance. Consent was informed and genuinely theirs. And the grief arrived exactly as described, and the text attributes it to the choosing. The conclusion is not that disclosure is worthless. The conclusion is narrower and far more practical: HEARING a cost and having ACCOUNTED for a cost are two different states of knowledge, and the distance between them is where most households live permanently. Israel was informed and unaccounted. So is nearly everyone reading this. Which produces the deliverable, and it is one of the plainest in the department. Write the itemised annual schedule of what every authority over your property actually takes. Each tax. Each levy. Each association fee. Each mandated insurance. Each required inspection, licence and filing fee. A real figure beside each line, and a total at the bottom. Almost no property-owning household has ever seen that total. They know every item individually — each one arrived with a notice, and each notice was read. The sum has never been assembled in one place, which means it has never been weighed. That is the Israel condition exactly, and the remedy is not more information. They had the information. The remedy is the total. Then add the question this chapter cannot answer for you, and the honesty here matters. Israel consented with full disclosure in hand. A great many modern obligations arrive AFTER a household is committed — a reassessment two years into ownership, a new levy voted by an association, a rule changed while you hold the asset. That is a different situation from theirs, with a different remedy, and blurring the two would let this lesson scold people for burdens they never chose. So the schedule gets a second column: what you consented to knowingly, and what was added to you afterwards. Only the first is your own account to settle. The second is the thing to watch, to document, and in some cases to contest — which is the subject of the next three sessions.',
