    stories: [
      {
        kind: 'parable', tone: 'solemn',
        title: 'The Easement Everybody Knew About',
        body: 'Everybody on that road knew the Harlan family had always used the gravel track across the back of the Pruitt place. Three generations had used it. The Pruitts had never minded. The Harlans had graded it twice at their own cost. When old Mr Pruitt was alive he used to wave from the porch. It was so thoroughly known that nobody had ever thought of it as a question. The property sold in an estate to a buyer from out of state, and in the spring there was a gate. The Harlans said there was an easement. The buyer asked to see it. It was not in the deed. It was not recorded anywhere in the county. It had never been written down, because everybody knew, and writing down what everybody knows feels like an insult to people who have been kind to you. The lawyer they hired was honest with them, which cost him the fee for a longer fight. He said they might well prevail on a use argument and it would take two years and cost more than the access was worth, and he was not confident. They sold the back forty in the autumn because it could no longer be reached with a truck. Nobody in this story is a villain. The buyer had bought a deed and read it. The Pruitts had been generous for sixty years. The Harlans had been grateful and had said so. What was missing was one sheet of paper that would have taken forty minutes and cost nothing but a conversation that three generations of good neighbours found too awkward to have.',
      },
      {
        kind: 'testimony', tone: 'hopeful',
        title: 'Ten Minutes on a Tuesday',
        body: 'I ran the drill because I was sure I would pass it. I am organised. I have a fire safe. I have a folder for every property and a naming convention for scans that I am, frankly, a little proud of. Deed: forty seconds. Survey: two minutes, and I had to look in two places. Insurance declarations: fine. Lien release on the note we paid off in 2019: I could not produce it. Not in ten minutes and not in ninety. I have a payoff letter. I do not have the recorded release, and after two hours I established that I never had it, because in 2019 I assumed the lender recorded it and nobody in that transaction ever told me otherwise. It took one phone call and eleven days to get it, and a small fee. That is the whole story and it is not dramatic, which is exactly why I tell it. The cost of finding that out on a Tuesday evening with nothing at stake was eleven days and forty dollars. The cost of finding it out at a closing table, with a buyer, is a different number and I have watched someone else pay it. What I would say to anyone who thinks they would pass: I thought I would pass. Run it anyway. Ten minutes. The item you cannot produce is never the one you were worried about.',
      },
    ],
    lesson: 'The most practical verse in Scripture about record-keeping is buried inside a genealogy, and it is worth digging out. The setting is the return from exile and the reconstitution of a community by register. Certain families arrive and run into an evidentiary problem stated without drama: "they could not shew their father’s house, and their seed, whether they were of Israel" (Ezra 2:59). Then the determination. "These sought their register among those that were reckoned by genealogy, but they were not found: therefore were they, as polluted, put from the priesthood" (Ezra 2:62). Read first what the text does NOT say, because the absence is the entire lesson. There is no allegation of fraud. There is no finding that the claim was untrue. Nothing in the passage suggests these men were impostors, and it is entirely possible — on the balance of it, likely — that they were exactly who they said they were. Sons of priests, with a real inheritance, whose papers had not survived a captivity. What is recorded is an EVIDENTIARY failure, not a moral one. The register was sought. It was not found. The consequence followed from the absence, and from nothing else. So state the principle plainly, because it will be resisted and it is nonetheless true: a right that cannot be documented is, at the moment of testing, a right that cannot be exercised. Moral entitlement and provable entitlement are different things, and they come apart more often than anyone likes. This is one of the places the Word shows them apart and does not soften it. Then keep the mercy that is actually in the text, because leaving it out would misrepresent both Yahweh and the governor. They are not ruled out for ever. They are not to eat of the most holy things "till there stood up a priest with Urim and with Thummim" (Ezra 2:63). The standing is SUSPENDED pending a means of settling it, not extinguished. A right unproven is not a right destroyed; it is a right you cannot use today, and the door is deliberately left open. Now the instrument, and it is a drill rather than a discussion. Identify the most valuable claim attached to your property. The deed. The boundary survey. The recorded easement. The cost basis for the improvements you have made over fifteen years. A tax exemption you rely on. The insurance and claims history. The lien release on the note you finished paying in 2019. Then PRODUCE the document. Today. From where you actually keep it. Inside ten minutes. Insist on the ten minutes, and know why it is the right figure rather than an arbitrary one: when a claim is challenged, a payer is rarely granted three weeks to go looking. The realistic window is short and it is set by somebody else. A document that exists somewhere is not the same thing as a document you can produce, and the difference between them only becomes visible under a clock. Expect the drill to fail on at least one item for most households, including careful ones. Treat that as the successful outcome of the session. The failure found tonight, with nothing at stake, is the same failure that would otherwise be found on the one day it costs something — and this department has now taught that shape in nine different forms. Close on the question this course keeps returning to, and it is not rhetorical. Which of your claims would survive being tested this week? And which of them have simply not been tested yet?',
